WEBVTT

00:00.350 --> 00:05.134
Rail engineering teams work across standards, maintenance instructions, change
notices and asset records.

00:05.134 --> 00:13.230
Before a change is accepted or work is authorised, the reviewer needs the
governing source, the correct revision and a defensible route

00:13.230 --> 00:14.702
back to the evidence.

00:14.702 --> 00:21.326
When those relationships are difficult to inspect, scarce engineering time is
spent reconstructing the document trail instead of

00:21.326 --> 00:22.430
judging the work.

00:23.430 --> 00:29.845
A single asset class can depend on infrastructure standards, rolling-stock
instructions, maintenance procedures, technical bulletins

00:29.845 --> 00:31.555
and local change records.

00:31.555 --> 00:33.693
One document may supersede another.

00:33.693 --> 00:36.259
A referenced clause may have moved.

00:36.259 --> 00:39.253
Terminology and effectivity can differ between revisions.

00:39.253 --> 00:41.818
The challenge is not simply search.

00:41.818 --> 00:46.950
It is establishing which source governed the question at the relevant time.

00:47.950 --> 00:53.291
Cortessa's target rail-site deployment gives each authorised engineer a
dedicated, self-sufficient box.

00:53.291 --> 01:00.413
Local standards, instructions, change records, ingestion, index, on-device
model and source viewer remain on that box.

01:00.413 --> 01:04.418
Audit and Ask run locally against the approved corpus.

01:04.418 --> 01:07.979
Author remains in development and requires further testing.

01:07.979 --> 01:12.430
The engineer controls the working context and every approved revision.

01:13.430 --> 01:21.085
Engineer boxes are designed to connect through infrastructure, rolling-stock
and standards-assurance departmental nodes to an on-site

01:21.085 --> 01:23.955
hub, entirely inside the air-gapped site.

01:23.955 --> 01:28.261
This is the target architecture, not a deployed-network claim.

01:28.261 --> 01:34.959
The offline contract and application foundations exist; authenticated
transport, key operations, scheduling, backup, recovery,

01:34.959 --> 01:37.830
assurance and rollout remain deployment work.

01:38.830 --> 01:41.735
Audit is the centre of the Cortessa workflow.

01:41.735 --> 01:47.544
It works across the bounded engineering corpus rather than waiting for one
question at a time.

01:47.544 --> 01:55.532
It can surface a reference the corpus does not hold, terminology that changes
between documents, an instruction that appears to rely on

01:55.532 --> 01:58.073
a superseded source, or uncertainty about effectivity.

01:58.073 --> 02:02.430
Each candidate is presented for investigation, not treated as an automatic
finding.

02:03.430 --> 02:07.791
Every audit result must lead back to the passage that created it.

02:07.791 --> 02:13.243
The reviewer can open the source, inspect surrounding wording and confirm the
revision and context.

02:13.243 --> 02:17.967
If the evidence is absent, Cortessa can refuse rather than fill the gap.

02:17.967 --> 02:24.872
The engineer decides whether the discrepancy is real, irrelevant or already
controlled, then records the outcome through the existing

02:24.872 --> 02:25.236
process.

02:25.236 --> 02:28.870
Verified shows provenance; it does not establish the engineering conclusion.

02:29.870 --> 02:37.191
Asset context, engineering risk, operational constraints and applicable
standards cannot be delegated to a language model.

02:37.191 --> 02:42.224
Cortessa can make the evidence route and uncertainty easier to inspect.

02:42.224 --> 02:49.544
The authorised engineer determines applicability, resolves apparent conflicts
and decides whether a change or instruction is

02:49.544 --> 02:50.002
acceptable.

02:50.002 --> 02:55.950
Cortessa does not approve engineering change, establish ROGS compliance or
control railway operations.

02:56.950 --> 03:03.899
An engineer-approved revision can carry signed identity, content hash, parent
lineage and approved audit context through its

03:03.899 --> 03:07.169
departmental node and, where authorised, the on-site hub.

03:07.169 --> 03:12.074
Conflicts remain explicit; the hub cannot approve or choose a governing
revision.

03:12.074 --> 03:15.752
Receipt does not certify or automatically index a document.

03:15.752 --> 03:23.110
The receiving engineer governs import and use, while the local Ledger supports
inspection without replacing the engineering record.

03:24.110 --> 03:28.972
Compare Cortessa with the current workflow using the same documents and tasks.

03:28.972 --> 03:35.860
Measure time to the governing source, reviewer effort, accepted and rejected
findings, false-finding burden, unanswered questions and

03:35.860 --> 03:36.265
traceability.

03:36.265 --> 03:39.506
Include stale references, conflicting wording and absent sources.

03:39.506 --> 03:41.937
Record corpus preparation and setup effort.

03:41.937 --> 03:49.230
The result should show where Cortessa helps, where it fails and what must
change before any wider use.

03:50.230 --> 03:55.750
Bring one bounded rail workflow, an agreed document set and the authorised
engineers.

03:55.750 --> 03:59.996
Test the evidence route and governed departmental exchange before expansion.

03:59.996 --> 04:03.818
Cortessa claims no rail deployment or completed site rollout.

04:03.818 --> 04:06.790
Start a conversation about a scoped evaluation.
